// Buff self-host port of `crates/buff-lang-debug-info/src/lib.rs` (P3.4).
//
// `buff-lang-debug-info` — Buff-span stack traces via `.buffmap` source-map
// sidecar + `std::panic` hook.
//
// # T24 — Stack Traces with Buff Spans
//
// Every modern scripting language that lowers to a lower-level runtime
// (TypeScript, Python, Dart, …) gives users stack traces in their own
// language, not the underlying IR. Buff lowers to Rust, so without a
// translation layer a panicked Buff program shows the user generated
// `<file>.rs:LINE:COL` paths and Rust-internal frames — useless for
// debugging the Buff source.
//
// This crate ships that translation layer in three pieces:
//
// 1. **Capture** ([`capture`]) — during codegen, walk the Buff AST +
//    formatted Rust source and build a [`SourceMap`] carrying the
//    bidirectional Rust-line ↔ Buff-(file,line,col,span) mapping plus
//    the originating Buff identifier names (function names especially).
// 2. **Format** ([`format`]) — serialise the [`SourceMap`] to a JSON
//    `.buffmap` sidecar file written next to the compiled binary. Both
//    forward (Rust → Buff) and reverse (Buff → Rust) lookup tables ship
//    in the file so the DAP server (T136) and `buff backtrace` can
//    consume it for offline work.
// 3. **Panic hook** ([`panic_hook`]) — register a `std::panic::set_hook`
//    interceptor at runtime that reads the `.buffmap` and walks the
//    Rust backtrace, remapping each frame to its Buff source location.
//    `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` is always preserved as an escape hatch (full
//    Rust trace printed AFTER the Buff trace).
//
// # Pipeline wiring
//
// ```text
//   buff-lang-codegen-rust::generate_rust(&[Decl]) -> String
//        │
//        ▼  capture::build_source_map(decls, &rust_source, buff_path)
//   buff-lang-debug-info::SourceMap
//        │
//        ▼  format::serialize(&source_map) -> String
//   <binary>.buffmap   (written alongside <binary>)
//        │
//        ▼  panic_hook::install("<binary>.buffmap")
//   std::panic::set_hook(...)
//        │
//        ▼  on panic: panic_hook::remap_panic_backtrace(...) -> Buff trace
//   stderr
// ```
//
// # Determinism
//
// All map/set types in this crate are [`BTreeMap`] / [`BTreeSet`] — never
// `HashMap`/`HashSet` — so the `.buffmap` JSON output is byte-identical
// across runs (project hard rule, see root AGENTS.md).
//
// Faithful 1:1 translation of the Rust reference. Buff has no `&` / `&mut`
// references (per porting §7b), no `PathBuf` (file paths are plain `String`,
// per the source_map.buff precedent), no `usize`/`u32` distinction (porting
// §7a collapses to `Int`), and no `impl Type { fn ... }` blocks as the
// idiom (per porting §8g the Rust impl block becomes a family of free
// functions taking the receiver as the first parameter; the receiver name
// follows the `<type_prefix>_<method_name>` convention from the corpus).
//
// Translation decisions:
//   * `//!` crate doc -> `//` file-level header block (porting §3).
//   * `pub mod capture; pub mod format; pub mod panic_hook;` -> deleted.
//     Buff has no `mod` keyword; sibling files in `self-host/debug-info/`
//     are auto-linked by the monolith loader (Phase 5, M7). Until M7
//     lands, multi-file ports are read as a flat namespace.
//   * `pub use capture::build_source_map;` (and friends) -> deleted. Buff
//     has no glob re-export; each sibling file's public symbols are
//     surfaced via their qualified `<file>_<symbol>` names directly.
//     M7 will resolve cross-file references; until then callers use the
//     prefixed form.
//   * `std::collections::BTreeMap<usize, BuffLocation>` ->
//     `Map<Int, BuffLocation>` (porting §7c — Buff `Map` is BTreeMap-backed,
//     deterministic iteration, matches the project hard rule).
//   * `std::path::PathBuf` -> `Option<String>` (Buff paths are UTF-8
//     strings; `Option<None>` mirrors the Rust `None` for "path not set").
//   * `buff_lang_error::{SourceId, Span}` -> `import` from the sibling
//     error port (`../error/span`). Buff's cross-file import resolves to
//     the same SourceId/Span types defined there.
//   * `impl SourceMap { fn new(); fn with_buff_file(); ... }` -> a family
//     of free functions `source_map_new`, `source_map_with_buff_file`, etc.
//     taking the receiver as the first parameter (`m: SourceMap`). The
//     Rust `&mut self` of `add_line_mapping` / `add_function` is implicit
//     (Buff mutates struct fields in place via `m.field = ...`).
//   * `impl Default for SourceMap` -> `source_map_default()` free function
//     (Buff has no `Default` trait; the convention from span.buff is a
//     `<type>_default` function that delegates to the regular constructor).
//   * `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]` dropped — Buff auto-derives
//     `Debug, Clone, PartialEq`; the rest are Rust host concerns.
//   * `usize` / `u32` -> `Int` (porting §7a — all unsigned widths collapse
//     to `Int`; document if precision matters, which it does not for
//     line/column/source_id values that fit in i64).
//
// Runtime intrinsics declared as extern stubs — actual linking deferred to
// the buff-lang-runtime crate. This file itself has NO runtime intrinsics
// (it is pure data + helper functions on that data); the extern stubs live
// in `capture.buff` (AST access), `format.buff` (JSON + filesystem), and
// `panic_hook.buff` (panic hook + backtrace).

import { Span, SourceId } from "../error/span"

// The bidirectional Rust-line ↔ Buff-location mapping that backs the
// `.buffmap` sidecar file.
//
// Built during codegen by [`build_source_map`] (see capture.buff). Forward
// lookup (Rust → Buff) is consulted by the panic hook at runtime; reverse
// lookup (Buff → Rust) is consumed by the DAP server (T136) +
// `buff backtrace`.
//
// All maps are [`BTreeMap`]s keyed by Rust line number so iteration order
// is deterministic — the same Buff program produces the same `.buffmap`
// JSON byte-for-byte across runs (project hard rule).
struct SourceMap:
    // Path of the originating `.buff` source file (recorded into the
    // `.buffmap` so offline consumers — `buff backtrace`, DAP — know
    // which file the mappings refer to without extra context).
    buff_file: Option<String>
    // Path of the generated `.rs` file (recorded for symmetry + for
    // offline consumers that need to cross-reference rustc diagnostics).
    rust_file: Option<String>
    // Rust line (1-based) → Buff location. Populated during codegen by
    // scanning the formatted Rust source for Buff identifier anchors
    // (function names). See [`build_source_map`] in capture.buff.
    rust_to_buff: Map<Int, BuffLocation>
    // Function-level mapping: Buff function name → `(Buff span, Rust
    // line range)`. Used by [`remap_panic_backtrace`] in panic_hook.buff
    // to resolve which Buff function a Rust frame is inside, even when
    // no per-line mapping exists for the exact Rust line of the frame.
    functions: Vector<FunctionAnchor>
    // Source ID of the originating `.buff` file. Stored so consumers
    // that round-trip the [`Span`] back through `buff_lang_error`'s
    // [`SourceMap`] (`lookup(id, offset)`) can resolve byte offsets to
    // `(line, col)` pairs.
    source_id: SourceId

// A Buff source location: byte span + the resolved 1-based `(line, col)`
// pair + the originating Buff identifier name (function name, etc.) when
// known.
//
// `line` and `col` are pre-computed during capture (via the canonical
// `buff_lang_error::SourceFile::lookup`) so consumers don't need to
// re-resolve byte offsets at runtime. The `name` field is the
// human-friendly identifier shown in stack traces (`helper`, `main`, …);
// `None` when the location has no associated identifier (e.g. an
// intermediate statement line).
struct BuffLocation:
    // 1-based line number in the originating `.buff` file.
    line: Int
    // 1-based column number in the originating `.buff` file.
    col: Int
    // Byte span in the originating `.buff` file. Round-trips through
    // [`buff_lang_error::SourceMap`] for diagnostic rendering.
    span: Span
    // Originating Buff identifier name when known (e.g. "helper",
    // "main"). `None` for locations that don't correspond to a named
    // declaration.
    name: Option<String>

// Function-level anchor: Buff function name + Buff span + Rust line
// range. Built by [`build_source_map`] in capture.buff for each top-level
// Buff function declaration.
//
// Consulted by [`remap_panic_backtrace`] in panic_hook.buff (via
// [`source_map_lookup_buff`]'s fallback path) when no exact per-line
// mapping exists for a Rust frame's line — the frame is attributed to
// whichever Buff function its Rust line falls inside.
struct FunctionAnchor:
    // Buff function name (e.g. "helper", "main").
    name: String
    // Byte span of the Buff function declaration.
    buff_span: Span
    // 1-based line of the Buff function declaration in the `.buff` file.
    buff_line: Int
    // 1-based column of the Buff function declaration.
    buff_col: Int
    // 1-based start line of the generated `fn <name>(...)` in the
    // formatted Rust source.
    rust_start_line: Int
    // 1-based end line (closing `}`) of the generated fn in the Rust
    // source.
    rust_end_line: Int
    // Cached Buff location used by [`source_map_lookup_buff`]'s
    // fallback path. `None` when the anchor carries no resolvable
    // Buff location (e.g. built manually without line/col context).
    buff_location: Option<BuffLocation>

// Create an empty source map with no file paths recorded.
func source_map_new() -> SourceMap:
    return SourceMap.new(
        buff_file: Option.None,
        rust_file: Option.None,
        rust_to_buff: Map.new(),
        functions: [],
        source_id: source_id_new(0),
    )

// Record the originating `.buff` file path + its [`SourceId`].
//
// Returns a new SourceMap (the receiver is consumed); matches the Rust
// builder pattern `Self -> Self`. Callers chain this with the other
// `with_*` functions.
func source_map_with_buff_file(m: SourceMap, path: String, source_id: SourceId) -> SourceMap:
    let updated = m
    updated.buff_file = Option.Some(path)
    updated.source_id = source_id
    return updated

// Record the generated `.rs` file path.
//
// Returns a new SourceMap (the receiver is consumed); matches the Rust
// builder pattern `Self -> Self`.
func source_map_with_rust_file(m: SourceMap, path: String) -> SourceMap:
    let updated = m
    updated.rust_file = Option.Some(path)
    return updated

// Record that a 1-based `rust_line` corresponds to a Buff
// [`BuffLocation`]. Later calls for the same `rust_line` win
// (the previous entry is overwritten).
func source_map_add_line_mapping(m: SourceMap, rust_line: Int, location: BuffLocation):
    m.rust_to_buff[rust_line] = location

// Record a function-level [`FunctionAnchor`] — name + Buff span +
// Rust line range. The anchor is consulted by
// [`remap_panic_backtrace`] in panic_hook.buff when no exact per-line
// mapping exists for a Rust frame's line (the frame falls inside
// the function's Rust line range).
func source_map_add_function(m: SourceMap, anchor: FunctionAnchor):
    m.functions = vector_push(m.functions, anchor)

// Look up the [`BuffLocation`] for a 1-based `rust_line`.
//
// Returns the exact match when `rust_line` was recorded via
// [`source_map_add_line_mapping`]. Otherwise, returns the closest
// recorded line **at or below** `rust_line` — this mirrors how
// `rustc`/panic locations point at the *start* of the statement that
// failed, so the nearest mapped statement above is the best
// candidate. As a final fallback, walks the [`functions`] list for any
// anchor whose Rust line range contains `rust_line` and returns its
// Buff location.
func source_map_lookup_buff(m: SourceMap, rust_line: Int) -> Option<BuffLocation>:
    let exact = m.rust_to_buff.get(rust_line)
    if exact != Option.None:
        return exact
    let nearest_below = source_map_scan_closest_below(m.rust_to_buff, rust_line)
    if nearest_below != Option.None:
        return nearest_below
    // Final fallback: function-level containment. The first function
    // whose [rust_start_line, rust_end_line] range contains rust_line
    // wins. functions is a Vec (declaration order, deterministic) so
    // the order matches AST order.
    return source_map_find_function_location(m.functions, rust_line)

// Inner scan: walk the map entries in ascending key order, keeping the
// largest key `<= rust_line`. Separated from `source_map_lookup_buff` so
// the `Option<BuffLocation>` initial value comes from a typed helper
// rather than an explicit annotation. Matches the source_map.buff
// precedent (lines 215-229 of self-host/error/source_map.buff).
func source_map_scan_closest_below(rust_to_buff: Map<Int, BuffLocation>, rust_line: Int) -> Option<BuffLocation>:
    let mut best_line = -1
    let mut best_loc = rust_to_buff.get(-1)  // sentinel None
    let entries = rust_to_buff.entries()
    let mut i = 0
    for i < entries.len():
        let (rl, loc) = entries[i]
        if rl <= rust_line:
            if rl > best_line:
                best_line = rl
                best_loc = Option.Some(loc)
        i = i + 1
    return best_loc

// Inner fallback: scan the function-anchor list for the first anchor
// whose [rust_start_line, rust_end_line] range contains `rust_line`,
// then return its `buff_location` field (which may itself be `None`).
func source_map_find_function_location(functions: Vector<FunctionAnchor>, rust_line: Int) -> Option<BuffLocation>:
    let mut i = 0
    for i < functions.len():
        let f = functions[i]
        if rust_line >= f.rust_start_line && rust_line <= f.rust_end_line:
            return f.buff_location
        i = i + 1
    return Option.None

// Look up the originating Buff identifier name for a 1-based
// `rust_line`, if any. Convenience wrapper around
// [`source_map_lookup_buff`] for the panic hook (which shows function
// names in stack traces).
func source_map_lookup_name(m: SourceMap, rust_line: Int) -> Option<String>:
    let loc = source_map_lookup_buff(m, rust_line)
    return source_map_extract_name(loc)

// Inner helper: pull the `name` field out of `Option<BuffLocation>`.
// Separated from `source_map_lookup_name` so the `Option<String>` return
// is threaded through a single named function rather than inlined
// `.map`/`.and_then` chains (the corpus style favours named helpers
// over inline closure pipelines — see source_map.buff).
func source_map_extract_name(loc: Option<BuffLocation>) -> Option<String>:
    return match loc {
        Some(l) => l.name,
        None => Option.None,
    }

// Returns `true` when no Rust-line ↔ Buff mappings have been
// recorded (line OR function level). Consumers can use this to
// decide whether to fall back to the raw Rust trace.
func source_map_is_empty(m: SourceMap) -> Bool:
    let line_empty = m.rust_to_buff.len() == 0
    let func_empty = m.functions.len() == 0
    if line_empty:
        return func_empty
    return false

// Vector push helper. Buff's Vector prelude exposes `.push()` but the
// corpus style (see source_map.buff's `map_*` helpers) wraps mutations
// in named functions for symmetry with the Map helpers. Returns the
// updated vector (Buff vectors are move-by-default, so this matches the
// `let v = vec![...]; v.push(x)` Rust shape: the receiver is consumed
// and the new value returned).
func vector_push(v: Vector<FunctionAnchor>, anchor: FunctionAnchor) -> Vector<FunctionAnchor>:
    let updated = v
    updated.push(anchor)
    return updated

// Default constructor — mirrors `impl Default for SourceMap`. Buff has
// no `Default` trait; the convention is a `<type>_default` function
// that delegates to the regular constructor (per span.buff precedent).
func source_map_default() -> SourceMap:
    return source_map_new()

// Construct a `SourceId` from a raw integer. Mirrors the Rust tuple-
// struct constructor `SourceId(n)` via the sibling error port's
// `source_id_new` helper. Re-bound here so callers of this module don't
// need a second import for the common case.
func source_id_new(value: Int) -> SourceId:
    return error_span_source_id_new(value)

// extern trampoline to the sibling error port's `source_id_new`.
// Per porting §6b, cross-file references go through `import` once M7
// lands; until then the Buff check resolver falls back to a flat
// namespace, so we forward through a stable local name. The Rust
// original imports `SourceId` from `buff_lang_error` directly; the
// Buff port mirrors the same intent through this trampoline.
extern "C" from "buff_lang_error" func error_span_source_id_new(value: Int) -> SourceId
